---
A ZML model describes its computation with tensors. Their shapes tell ZML
how many values to expect, and dimension tags give meaningful names to axes.
The examples follow the revision in the [ZML quickstart]($link.page('zml/quickstart')).

# [Tensors]($heading.id('tensors'))

A `zml.Tensor` represents a symbolic value, such as a model weight, an input,
or an intermediate result. `zml.Tensor.init(.{ 2, 3 }, .f32)` declares a
tensor with two rows, three columns, and 32-bit floating-point elements.
During compilation, operations in `forward` describe how the model computes
its output. At runtime, `zml.Buffer` holds the concrete weights, inputs, and
results for the selected backend. See the
[execution diagram]($link.page('zml').ref('follow-the-model-lifecycle')).

# [Shapes]($heading.id('shapes'))

`tensor.shape()` returns a `zml.Shape`. It records the element type and
ordered dimension sizes. The **rank** is the number of axes. For a matrix
of shape `f32[2, 3]`, axis `0` has size `2` and axis `1` has size `3`.
The [Shape API reference]($link.page('zml/api').ref('shape')) lists the
calls for reading these values.

| Shape | Rank | Elements |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `f32[]` | 0 (scalar) | 1 |
| `f32[1]` | 1 (vector) | 1 |
| `f32[2, 3]` | 2 (matrix) | 6 |

Use `zml.Shape.scalar(.f32)` for the scalar and `zml.Shape.init(.{1}, .f32)`
for the one-element vector. Call `rank()` to count the axes, `dim(0)` to read
the size of the first axis of a non-scalar, and `dtype()` to read the element
type.

# [Dimension tags]($heading.id('dimension-tags'))

A **dimension tag** names an axis. On the weight matrix below, `.output`
names the first axis and `.feature` names the second. Pass one tag per axis
to `withTags` in the existing axis order. Tagging preserves the order,
sizes, and element type of the tensor.

For a weight matrix tagged in this order, `weights.axis(.feature)` returns
`1`, the axis index. `weights.dim(.feature)` returns `3`, the size of that
axis. Named selectors let a calculation refer to the feature axis even when
it occupies different positions in two tensors.

# [A dot product over a named axis]($heading.id('tagged-dot-product'))

This `forward` function expects weights of shape `f32[2, 3]` and an input of
shape `f32[3]`. It runs as part of model compilation.

```zig
const zml = @import("zml");

pub fn forward(weights: zml.Tensor, input: zml.Tensor) zml.Tensor {
    const tagged_weights = weights.withTags(.{ .output, .feature });
    const tagged_input = input.withTags(.{.feature});
    return tagged_weights.dot(tagged_input, .feature);
}
```

`dot` selects `.feature` in each tensor and sums the products across its
three elements for each weight row. The result has shape `f32[2]` and keeps
the `.output` tag. The contracted axes must have the same size. With batched
inputs, `dot` pairs any other tags shared by both tensors as batch axes.
